Subject: [#94-9-39] Water filters - small enough

> In terms of micron level filters, what does this translate to!
Sorry,

	1 micron = 1e-6 (one e minus 6) meters.

	To remove  
		Giardia    5 micron filter (absolute) as opposed to nominal
		All bacteria  .1 micron filter (abs)
		Virus	      .005 micron filter (abs) 

	Filters ordered from somebody like Osmonics are usually spec
with  2 ratings an absolute and a nominal.
	The nominal means a partical of size N microns will not get 
THROUGH the walls.
	The absolute means a partical of size N microns will not get
INTO the walls.

	By the way, when I started this side thread I was reacting to the
fact that someone was writing "small" enough instead of a more accurate
N meters or N microns.  I justed looked in the dictionary and it said:

	micron
		A unit of length equal to 1 millionth of a meter 
	        [ < Gk  mikros, small ]

